[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: The rules become executable in five milestones, plumbing before migration

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-EXECUTABLE-RULES-ROADMAP
TITLE: The rules become executable in five milestones, plumbing before migration
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: accepted
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The fifth milestone is verified, or a milestone's review changes the order or
the number of milestones.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
The rules of the design system become executable in five milestones, in this
order:

1. The grammar, generated from a typed Nix option surface, with a validation
   gate at commit time and in CI.
2. A command-line tool that is the only writer of sdoc files, with direct
   edits denied.
3. The behaviour model -- states, moves, gates, triggers, flows -- defined in
   Nix and rendered so it can be looked at.
4. That behaviour wired into the tool and into checkpoints at command, commit
   and merge time, with strictdoc's own hooks as the backstop.
5. This system's own semantics -- fingerprints, signing, the ripple, DEPTH --
   migrated onto that plumbing.

Every milestone is consumer-facing, so its option surface or verb list is
reviewed by the operator before the milestone is implemented. Milestones one
and two are separate slices; each milestone normally runs in its own session,
and the operator may continue from one to the next in the same session when
the first goes well. Migration waits for the plumbing. Whether a finished
milestone is worth integrating sooner is the operator's call.

The planning session that produced these nodes stays open, to re-slot a
milestone whose timing was wrong.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
In the operator's words, the activity began because a model produces sdoc in
incorrect formats, nothing validates a write when it happens, and the node
lifecycle could not be reasoned about from prose. Milestones one and two end
the first two problems without waiting on any lifecycle decision; milestone
three is where the lifecycle becomes something that can be looked at; only
after four does a rule run rather than get read, which is what
INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE asks for. Ordering migration last keeps the instance's
open decisions -- who signs, whether DEPTH may regress, agent identity -- from
blocking the plumbing.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Accepted by the operator on 2026-08-25 with one expectation stated: design
happens inside each step and may reshape that step. The plan was deliberately
not designed in full up front, to move faster through this part; a step's
slice is a starting shape, not a finished contract, until its own review.

The evidence behind every milestone is the SPIKE-EXECUTABLE-RULES assessment
and the fold-in review of two outside research documents that followed it,
both held in the operator's working area outside the repository.

A step zero precedes milestone one: SLICE-STRICTDOC-OVERLAY, the overlay that
keeps strictdoc current from upstream's own flake. Not a milestone -- it has no
consumer surface to review -- but every milestone runs on it.
<<<
